RTP reassembly: charge a lost packet to ONE frame instead of two.

extern/libpeer is a git SUBMODULE, so its sources must not be edited in
place: the next `git submodule update` would throw the changes away.  This
patch is applied to the checked out tree by
tools/apply-libpeer-switch-patches.sh, right before libpeer is built (it is
the tenth patch of that series, after 0001-switch-webrtc-fixes,
0002-no-stdout-log, 0003-h265-depacketizer, 0004-dtls-diagnostics,
0005-ice-roles-prflx-and-timing, 0006-dtls-fingerprint-and-verify,
0007-ice-timeout-and-verify-none, 0008-dtls-bio-remb-and-candidate-dedup and
0009-sctp-init-and-diagnostics).

*** READ THIS FIRST: this patch is NOT the fix for the 2026-10-23 failure ***
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

The failure in question ("0 key frames, 0 decoded frames, 2261 access units
delivered, 69 bytes per unit on average") was NOT caused by the reassembler:

  * the device report says packetsLost = 0, and packetsLost IS
    vstats.sequence_gaps (engine/source/webrtc_backend_libpeer.cc) -- so the
    session had ZERO sequence gaps;
  * the access unit rate was exactly 30.0/s, i.e. not a single frame was
    missing either.

=> the chain "a hole => the access unit is damaged => the whole frame is
dropped" NEVER FIRED in that run.  This patch is kept because it is a real
(but different) bug, not because it explains the failure.

What
---

  * ONE lost packet costs ONE frame, not two.

    The old code charged a hole to the access unit TWICE: once where the
    hole is detected (right: the access unit that is open NOW is the one
    that contains the hole) and once again AFTER
    rtp_decoder_begin_access_unit() (wrong: by then that access unit has
    been flushed and the NEXT frame has started -- and the next frame is
    complete).  The second, unconditional assignment is GONE now, in the
    H.264 path and in the H.265 path.

  * The two paths are TEXTUALLY IDENTICAL.

    The H.264 and the H.265 gap handling were compared side by side
    (rtp_decode_h264() and rtp_decode_h265()): both were
    "if (sequence_gap) { sequence_gaps++; au_damaged = 1; ... }" plus the
    same second unconditional assignment -- i.e. they behaved IDENTICALLY,
    so there was no behavioural asymmetry to fix (the claim that the H.264
    path was "unconditional" while the H.265 path was "conditional" does
    not hold for this tree: both assignments sit inside "if (sequence_gap)").
    The two blocks now carry the SAME comment, so they cannot drift apart
    again -- and the next reader does not have to re-derive that.

What this patch deliberately does NOT do any more
---------------------------------------------------

  An earlier version of this patch ALSO

    * replaced the flat 30 ms gap hold with an RTT driven one
      (2 x RTT + 20 ms, clamped to 30..400 ms) and
    * grew the reorder window from 128 to 512 slots (and the hold limit
      from 64 to 256 packets).

  Both were removed on purpose:

    * they were never triggered: the session had no sequence gap at all;
    * the window costs 8x the memory (256 KB -> 1 MB per peer) and the
      hold pushes the latency to 300 ms;
    * our own discipline is "one variable per round": with the PLI trigger
      (0011) and the codec fix (enableWrappedH265) in flight, a third
      change in the reassembler would make the next device report
      unreadable ("was it the PLI? was it the codec? was it the window?").

  => struct RtpDecoder keeps its ORIGINAL LAYOUT: this patch adds NO field
  and changes NO constant.  (0011 adds four counters to it -- that one DOES
  change the layout, so libpeer.a has to be rebuilt for 0011, not for this
  patch.)

How the criterion changes (AGENTS.md 6.2, rule 8)
---------------------------------------------------

  * a session WITH a hole: one lost packet used to cost two frames, now it
    costs one -- access_units_dropped drops by one per hole;
  * a session WITHOUT a hole (the 2026-10-23 run): NOTHING changes.  That
    is the point: this patch must not be able to change the next report
    except through the counters that belong to the PLI work.

A host-side self test of the rule itself (and of the source invariants this
patch establishes) is next to this file:
